  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThe overall tendency for ridging to shift farther west Thursday \nand Friday, allowing a northwesterly flow to set up aloft, remains\nevident across the models. This has the benefit of both shifting \nthe warmest portion of the summertime airmass away from our area, \nand also providing an avenue for weak areas of vorticity to round \nthe ridge and skirt South Central Texas, bringing some potential \nfor showers and thunderstorms. This looks most favored Thursday, \nwhen the Z700 flow becomes northwesterly as a shortwave trough \napproaches. Rain chances Thursday are low to medium, from about 20\nto 50 percent. Rain chances could continue into Friday if this \nenergy sticks around and enough surface heating occurs beneath the\nslight mid-level baroclinicity, bringing another shot of isolated\nshower and storm activity Friday afternoon. Given the broader \nenvironment, any convection that develops will probably be high- \nbased with the strongest cells providing a threat for gusty winds \nand lightning. The likelihood for rain is expected to decrease \nfrom north to south. The aforementioned displacement in the \nhottest air and opportunities for cloud cover favor highs in the \nlatter half of the week lowering slightly below advisory criteria,\nthough it would not take much of a downtrend in rain chances to \nkeep temperatures as hot as they've been. Additionally, higher dew\npoints could keep heat indices comparably hot regardless of \nvariations in the ambient temperature.\n\nOver the weekend, becomes a little more diffuse and broadens, \nresulting in a less favorable flow aloft for dynamically supported\nconvection. This will bring rain chances down with drier \nconditions expected to prevail, though the slightly weaker \nstrength of the ridge implies that a few isolated seabreeze \nshowers could break through on occasion in the Coastal Plains. \nTemperatures remain hot and will likely continue to straddle 100F \nfor highs into early next week.",
